==English==

===Etymology===
From Old (and modern) French '''''[[solliciter]]''''', from Latin '''''[[sollicitare]]''''' ‘stir, disturb’, later ‘look after’, from '''''[[sollicitus]]''''' ‘[[agitate]]d’, from '''''[[sollus]]''''' ‘whole’ + past participle of '''''[[ciere]]''''' ‘put in motion’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sə-lĭs'ĭt}}
* {{IPA| /səˈlɪsɪt/}}
* {{audio|en-us-solicit.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɪsɪt}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To persistently [[endeavor]] to obtain an object, or [[bring about]] an event.
# To [[woo]]; to [[court]].
# To persuade or [[incite]] one to commit some act, especially illegal or sexual behavior.
# To offer to perform sexual activity, especially when for a [[payment]].
# To make a [[petition]].
# {{archaic}} To [[disturb]] or trouble; to [[harass]].

